    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d773">

                <pb facs="0808" n="796" sameAs="frus:frus1942v05/pg796"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">840.51 Frozen Credits/6673: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-06-06T16:00:00-03:00">June 6, 1942—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1921. The Bank of Brazil has received a telegraphic request from the Bank of
                    International Settlements, Basel, Switzerland, to transfer to the Banco Central
                    in Buenos Aires the 503 kilos of gold held in the Bank of Brazil for its
                    account.</p>
                <p>The Bank of Brazil has replied: “We believe that Brazilian authorities would not
                    permit exportation of the gold during the emergency.”</p>
                <p>The Director of Exchange<anchor n="51" xml:id="d773-f1"/> says “This is a polite
                    way of saying no.”</p>
                <p>The gold was imported into Brazil in 1941 and turned over to the Bank of Brazil
                    by the French Embassy here.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
